cousins attended this morning's out of  the darkness walk at sunset park mary  jane belliesa shares how the event  provides mental health support and  suicide prevention  shining a light on mental health that's  the goal for the out of the darkness  community walk it's all to support  suicide prevention students from desert  oasis say this community walk couldn't  have come at a better time as the  effects of the pandemic has increased  the feelings of loneliness and isolation  our friend i think over a year ago now  she committed suicide  during quarantine we had a mental health  week and they gave us options like this  they tell people to come to suicide  walks and they give us mental health  weeks according to the american  foundation for suicide prevention  suicide is the third leading cause of  death in ages 10 to 35 years old local  leaders and a number of community  resources in attendance saturday hope  this event will bring awareness and  address the stigma surrounding mental  health  just talk to people because you never  know who's feeling the same way as you  or who can relate to you for siblings  denise and meliss they are walking for  their brother scott and while they say  the pain of losing a loved one may never  go away  very hard even  eight years later  to process it still it's just such a  wound on your heart for the family  connecting and helping others is their  way of giving back this gives us a  chance to celebrate him  and to have the beautiful memories and  and then to reach out to others who may  be suffering mary jane balleza live  local now  the event also included opening remarks  from governor sislek as you saw there an  honor bead ceremony and a mile walk  around the park to honor those we have  lost to suicide
 

